Dhara-Bench repository licensing
===============================

This repository uses different terms for its original software and original
annotations. It is not offered under a single licence covering every file.

1. Original software: MIT

   Original project software and configuration in scripts/ and associated
   software instructions in README.md are licensed under the MIT License.
   The full text is in LICENSE-CODE.

   Third-party dependencies and any separately identified third-party code
   retain their own licences; the MIT grant does not relicense them.

2. Original annotations: CC BY 4.0

   Contributor-authored question wording, category labels and definitions,
   question-to-chunk relevance links, and conjugate evidence-group annotations
   are licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International.
   This covers those original contributions in "Dhara Bench eval set.json",
   the README's annotation descriptions, and copies of that annotation
   content in metric_outputs/. The full text and attribution information are
   in LICENSE-DATA.

   The grant covers rights held by the Dhara-Bench contributors in their
   original contributions. It does not claim exclusive rights in facts,
   identifiers, or numerical scores, or grant rights in third-party text
   reproduced within a question or another record.

3. Source-derived material and other third-party content

   Neither project licence applies to source-derived corpus text in
   chunks.json or grants rights in underlying works, editions, translations,
   commentaries, or third-party quotations elsewhere in the repository.
   Those materials retain their applicable rights and terms. Public-domain
   material remains in the public domain.

   source_metadata.csv documents sources and URLs, not verified permissions.
   THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md records the current provenance and rights-status
   information. The repository's project licences do not establish a right
   to redistribute material whose source permissions are unresolved.

These repository licences do not select a licence for the associated paper.
